WEBVTT

00:00.496 --> 00:02.307
- So I have some example questions here

00:02.307 --> 00:03.998
from Khan Academy on arc measure.

00:03.998 --> 00:05.839
And like always, I encourage
you to pause the video

00:05.839 --> 00:07.167
after you see each of these questions,

00:07.167 --> 00:09.333
and try to solve them before I do.

00:10.051 --> 00:11.918
So this first question says
what is the arc measure,

00:11.918 --> 00:15.651
in degrees, of arc AC on circle P below.

00:16.181 --> 00:17.965
So this is point A,

00:17.965 --> 00:19.887
that is point C,

00:19.887 --> 00:21.921
and when they're talking about arc AC,

00:21.921 --> 00:23.509
since they only have two letters here,

00:23.509 --> 00:25.543
we can assume that it's
going to be the minor arc.

00:25.543 --> 00:26.574
When we talk about the minor arc.

00:26.574 --> 00:30.055
There's two potential arcs that
connect point A and point C.

00:30.529 --> 00:32.591
There's the one here on the left,

00:32.591 --> 00:34.764
and then there's the one,

00:34.764 --> 00:36.881
there is the one on the right.

00:37.160 --> 00:41.450
And since C isn't exactly
straight down from A,

00:41.450 --> 00:42.843
it's a little bit to the right,

00:42.843 --> 00:44.682
the shorter arc,

00:44.682 --> 00:46.772
the arc with the smaller length,

00:46.772 --> 00:48.443
or the minor arc is going to be this one

00:48.443 --> 00:51.006
that I'm depicting here
right on the right.

00:51.898 --> 00:54.294
So what is this arc measure going to be?

00:54.991 --> 00:57.220
Well, the measure of
this arc is going to be

00:57.220 --> 01:00.425
exactly the same thing as, in degrees,

01:00.425 --> 01:03.767
as the measure of the central
angle that intercepts the arc.

01:04.353 --> 01:06.859
So that central angle, let me
do it in a different color,

01:06.859 --> 01:08.753
I'll do it in this blue color,

01:09.227 --> 01:13.294
that central angle is angle C, P, A.

01:14.187 --> 01:17.084
Angle C, P, A, and the
measure of that central angle

01:17.084 --> 01:20.537
is going to be 70
degrees plus 104 degrees.

01:20.537 --> 01:22.962
It's going to be this whole
thing right over there.

01:22.962 --> 01:25.273
So it's going to be 174 degrees.

01:25.887 --> 01:29.090
One hundred and seventy four degrees,

01:29.090 --> 01:32.098
that's the arc measure,
in degrees, of arc AC.

01:32.517 --> 01:33.853
Let's keep doing these.

01:33.853 --> 01:35.160
So let me do another one.

01:35.599 --> 01:36.780
So,

01:37.526 --> 01:40.488
this next one asks us,

01:40.488 --> 01:42.378
in the figure below,

01:42.378 --> 01:45.188
in the figure below, segment AD--

01:45.188 --> 01:47.052
so this is point A,

01:47.052 --> 01:48.815
this is point D,

01:48.815 --> 01:51.711
so segment AD is this one right over here.

01:51.711 --> 01:54.103
Let me see if I can draw that.

01:54.103 --> 01:56.137
That's AD right over there,

01:56.137 --> 01:59.925
AD and CE are diameters of the circle.

02:00.093 --> 02:01.486
So let me draw CE,

02:01.486 --> 02:04.022
so CE is,

02:04.022 --> 02:07.170
we're going to connect point C and E.

02:07.170 --> 02:08.898
These are diameters.

02:08.898 --> 02:10.681
So, let me,

02:10.681 --> 02:11.850
so they go straight.

02:11.850 --> 02:14.107
Whoops, I'm using the
wrong tool, let me...

02:14.107 --> 02:16.280
So those are, somehow I should,

02:16.280 --> 02:17.537
alright.

02:17.537 --> 02:21.546
So, those are di--

02:21.546 --> 02:23.859
whoops, how did that happen?

02:23.859 --> 02:26.976
So let me, somehow my pen got

02:26.976 --> 02:29.205
really big, alright.

02:29.205 --> 02:31.880
That'll be almost there, ok.

02:31.880 --> 02:35.670
So CE, there you go.

02:35.670 --> 02:39.235
So those are both
diameters of the circle P.

02:39.821 --> 02:44.307
What is the arc measure of
AB, of arc AB in degrees?

02:44.307 --> 02:47.288
So arc AB, once again
there's two potential arcs

02:47.288 --> 02:50.128
that connect point A and B.

02:50.965 --> 02:52.107
There's the minor arc,

02:52.107 --> 02:53.361
and since this only has two letters

02:53.361 --> 02:54.615
we'll assume it's the minor arc.

02:54.615 --> 02:56.398
It's going to be this one over here.

02:56.398 --> 02:57.792
There's a major arc,

02:57.792 --> 02:59.324
but to not the major arc
they would've said something

02:59.324 --> 03:02.835
like A, E, B or A, D, B

03:02.835 --> 03:06.150
or arc A, C, B to make us go this kind of,

03:06.150 --> 03:08.352
this long way around.

03:08.352 --> 03:10.274
But this is arc AB, so we,

03:10.274 --> 03:11.918
in order to find the arc measure,

03:11.918 --> 03:13.540
we just really have to find the

03:13.540 --> 03:15.021
measure of this central angle.

03:15.021 --> 03:17.933
This is the central angle
that intercepts that arc,

03:17.933 --> 03:20.538
or you can even say it
defines that arc in some way.

03:20.538 --> 03:22.403
So how can we figure out this angle?

03:22.403 --> 03:23.839
And this one's a little bit trickier.

03:24.764 --> 03:25.811
Well, the key to,

03:25.811 --> 03:29.954
the key here is to realize
that this 93 degree angle,

03:30.531 --> 03:33.871
it is vertical to this
whole angle right over here.

03:34.771 --> 03:37.121
And we know from geometry,

03:37.121 --> 03:39.649
which we're still learning as
we do this example problem,

03:39.649 --> 03:42.658
that vertical angles are going
to have the same measure.

03:42.969 --> 03:44.859
So if this one on, this one is 93 degrees,

03:44.859 --> 03:46.681
then this entire blue one right over here

03:46.681 --> 03:48.794
is also gonna be, let me write it,

03:48.794 --> 03:52.076
this is also gonna be 93 degrees.

03:52.076 --> 03:55.949
So 93 degrees, that's gonna
be made up of this red angle,

03:55.949 --> 03:58.512
that we care about, and the 38 degrees.

03:58.512 --> 04:02.553
So this red one, which is the
measure of the central angle,

04:02.553 --> 04:05.339
it's also the arc measure of arc AB,

04:05.339 --> 04:07.456
is going to be 93 minus,

04:07.456 --> 04:10.299
93 degrees minus 38 degrees.

04:10.299 --> 04:11.942
So what is that going to be?

04:11.942 --> 04:14.365
Let's see, 93,

04:14.365 --> 04:15.591
I can write degrees there,

04:15.591 --> 04:18.517
minus 38 degrees,

04:18.517 --> 04:20.161
that is going to be equal to,

04:20.161 --> 04:22.640
let's see if it was 93 minus 40

04:22.640 --> 04:25.148
it would be, it would be 53,

04:25.148 --> 04:26.179
it's gonna be two more,

04:26.179 --> 04:27.737
it's gonna be 55 degrees.

04:28.769 --> 04:30.829
Fifty five degrees,

04:30.829 --> 04:32.081
and we are done.

04:32.081 --> 04:34.561
This angle right here is 55 degrees.

04:34.561 --> 04:36.567
If you were to add this angle meausre,

04:36.567 --> 04:39.047
plus 38 degrees, you would get 93 degrees,

04:39.047 --> 04:41.271
and that has the same
measure because it's vertical

04:41.271 --> 04:42.834
with this angle right over here,

04:42.834 --> 04:44.924
with angle D, P, E.

04:44.924 --> 04:46.816
Alright, let's do one more of these.

04:48.042 --> 04:50.327
So, we have

04:50.327 --> 04:52.027
in the figure below,

04:52.027 --> 04:53.281
and it doesn't quite fit on the page,

04:53.281 --> 04:55.064
but we'll scroll down in a second,

04:55.064 --> 04:59.073
AB is the diameter of circle P,

04:59.520 --> 05:01.525
is the diameter of circle P.

05:01.972 --> 05:04.535
Alright, so AB is the diameter,

05:04.535 --> 05:05.871
let me label that.

05:06.429 --> 05:09.158
So AB is the diameter.

05:09.158 --> 05:10.802
It's going straight across,

05:10.802 --> 05:13.588
straight across the circle.

05:13.908 --> 05:18.825
What is the arc measure
of A, B, C in degrees?

05:19.211 --> 05:21.661
So A, B, C.

05:21.661 --> 05:23.556
So they're making us
go the long way around.

05:23.556 --> 05:25.534
This is a major arc they're talking about.

05:25.534 --> 05:26.760
Let me draw it.

05:26.760 --> 05:29.853
Arc A, what is the arc measure of

05:29.853 --> 05:31.776
arc A, B, C.

05:31.776 --> 05:33.475
So we're going the long way around.

05:33.475 --> 05:35.314
So it's a major arc.

05:35.314 --> 05:37.070
So what is that going to be.

05:37.070 --> 05:39.048
Well it's going to be in degrees,

05:39.048 --> 05:41.528
the same measure as the angle,

05:41.528 --> 05:43.896
as the central angle that intercepts it.

05:43.896 --> 05:45.428
So it's going to be the same thing

05:45.428 --> 05:48.270
as this central angle right over here.

05:48.270 --> 05:50.945
Well, what is that
central angle going to be?

05:50.945 --> 05:52.924
Well, since we know that
this is the diameter,

05:52.924 --> 05:54.345
since AB is the diameter,

05:54.345 --> 05:57.322
we know that this part of it

05:58.660 --> 06:00.527
is going to 180 degrees.

06:00.527 --> 06:02.645
We're going halfway around the circle.

06:02.645 --> 06:04.177
One hundred eighty degrees.

06:04.177 --> 06:07.103
And so if we wanna look
at this whole angle,

06:07.103 --> 06:11.309
the angle that intercepts
the major arc A, B, C,

06:11.309 --> 06:15.851
is going to be 180
degrees plus 69 degrees.

06:15.851 --> 06:19.027
So we're going to have 180 degrees,

06:19.027 --> 06:21.451
plus 69 degrees

06:21.451 --> 06:23.680
which is equal to, what is that,

06:23.680 --> 06:27.156
249,

06:27.156 --> 06:29.996
249 degrees.

06:29.996 --> 06:34.008
That's the arc measure of
this major arc A, B, C.
